# The settings of the Dispatcher project (hosted deployment, docs/deployment-vercel.md, section 3.2).
# On Vercel they are set in the project's Environment Variables for the Production environment, not in a file;
# this file lists every name the Dispatcher reads, with a placeholder and what it means. Never commit a real
# value. A secret is created with `openssl rand -hex 32` (at least 32 characters, different from every
# other secret). A refusal at start names the variable, never its value.
#
# Variables of the App project that must match these: RUNNER_TOKEN, RUNNER_WEBHOOK_SECRET (= the signing
# secret below), RUNNER_WEBHOOK_KEY_ID (= the key id below), RUNNER_DISPATCH_MAX_RUNNING (= the pool cap below).
# --- The App ---
# Required. The App's public address, https, no path, query or credentials. The Worker inside the VM calls it
# too, so it must be reachable from the internet.
RUNNER_API_URL=https://reviews.example.com
# Required. The dispatcher credential: what this Dispatcher claims jobs with. Secret. The same value as RUNNER_TOKEN
# of the App project (and of `RUNNER_TOKEN` in Infisical /ci, the canary's).
RUNNER_TOKEN=
# Required. The pool cap, 1 to 1000: how many launches may be in progress (each running job is one VM). The same
# number as RUNNER_DISPATCH_MAX_RUNNING of the App.
DISPATCHER_MAX_RUNNING=2
# --- The wake-up call from the App ---
# Required. The id of the signing key (a-z, 0-9, _ and -, at most 32 characters). The App's RUNNER_WEBHOOK_KEY_ID
# (default k1).
DISPATCHER_SIGNING_KEY_ID=k1
# Required. The signing secret. Secret. At least 32 characters, different from RUNNER_TOKEN. The App's
# RUNNER_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
DISPATCHER_SIGNING_SECRET=
# Optional, both or neither. The key being replaced, accepted while keys rotate (docs/deployment-vercel.md, 5.2).
# DISPATCHER_SIGNING_KEY_ID_PREVIOUS=
# DISPATCHER_SIGNING_SECRET_PREVIOUS=
# --- The golden snapshot (docs/golden-snapshot.md): the two values of the record the snapshot workflow printed ---
# Required for a working deployment; unset, the Dispatcher has no Launcher and claims nothing. Set both or neither.
# The snapshot id (snapshotId of the record).
SANDBOX_SNAPSHOT_ID=
# The Worker image id in that snapshot: sha256: and 64 hex characters (imageDigest of the record). The Launcher
# refuses a VM whose image has another id. Always change the two together.
SANDBOX_IMAGE_DIGEST=
# --- What it serves and how ---
# Optional. The engines to serve: pr-agent, opencode or both (default). pr-agent for a Diff-only deployment: no
# capability probe and no canary VM.
# DISPATCHER_ENGINES=pr-agent,opencode
# Optional. How long a Worker may live, in seconds, 60 to 21600 (default 1200). Keep it plus the launch (at most
# 300 seconds) under the function limit of 1800 seconds.
# DISPATCHER_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1200
# Optional. The VM size and the container memory per Worker kind, JSON, for example
# {"dynamic":{"launcher":{"vcpus":2},"container":{"memory":{"limit":"3g","reservation":"2g"}}}}
# Without it both kinds get 1 vCPU / 2 GB and a container limit of 1536m (docs/deployment-vercel.md, section 6).
# DISPATCHER_WORKER_CONFIG=
# Optional. Sent as X-Runner-Id; the App counts the running jobs of the pool by it (default dispatcher).
# DISPATCHER_POOL_ID=dispatcher
# Optional. How long the container must run before the Worker counts as having taken over, 1 to 120 (default 20).
# SANDBOX_HANDOVER_SECONDS=20
# Optional. Seconds added to the job's timeout for the VM's own timeout, the backstop that ends a VM nobody
# stopped: 180 to 3600, at least 150 above the hand-over time (default 300).
# SANDBOX_VM_TIMEOUT_MARGIN_SECONDS=300
# --- The capability probe (Dynamic-Worker only; dispatcher/README.md, "The capability probe") ---
# Optional. How long a probe's answer counts before a new canary VM is made, 60 to 86400 (default 3600).
# DISPATCHER_PROBE_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# Optional. A forced probe (POST /probe) within this many seconds of the last answer is served from it, 0 to 600
# (default 60; 0 runs every one).
# DISPATCHER_PROBE_REUSE_SECONDS=60
# Optional. How long a probe may take, the canary VM included, 30 to 600 (default 150).
# DISPATCHER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=150
# Optional, only read when the Dispatcher serves opencode. The operator's Dynamic configuration for the container of a
# Dynamic job and for the canary: a JSON object on one line (at most 4096 characters), and a plain positive decimal.
# Normally unset (docs/dynamic-review.md, "The model").
# RUNNER_DYNAMIC_CAPABILITIES=
# RUNNER_DYNAMIC_MAX_COST_USD=
# --- The reaper (docs/deployment-vercel.md, 5.5) ---
# Required with the snapshot (the route exists only then). The secret Vercel's cron sends to GET /api/cron/reap-sandboxes
# every 5 minutes, which stops the VMs that outlived their job. Secret. At least 32 characters, different from
# RUNNER_TOKEN and the signing secrets, and a value of its own (not the App project's CRON_SECRET). Unset: every caller
# gets 401 and nothing is reaped.
CRON_SECRET=
# --- Platform access ---
# Optional, all three or none. Access by access token. With none of them the Dispatcher uses the OIDC token of its own
# Vercel project, which is the better choice (nothing to rotate).
# VERCEL_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
# VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN: provided by the platform inside a function; never set it
# --- The Node entry only (`pnpm start`, not used on Vercel) ---
# Optional. How long launches may finish after SIGTERM, 0 to 600 (default 60).
# DISPATCHER_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_SECONDS=60
# Optional. The port of the Node entry (default 8080).
# PORT=8080
# --- Not settings of the deployed Dispatcher: the deploy canary (`pnpm probe:canary`, workflow dispatcher-canary.yml) ---
# The hosts the canary may send the dispatcher credential to, comma separated exact host names: for a person at a
# terminal. The release pipeline does not use a list: it passes --verify-vercel, and the Vercel API says whether the
# staged host is the Dispatcher project's own deployment (docs/deployment-vercel.md, section 2.1).
# CANARY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=dispatcher.example.com
# --verify-vercel reads these three (in the pipeline they come from Infisical /ci): a Vercel access token, the team id and
# the Dispatcher project's id.
# VERCEL_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=
# The Deployment Protection bypass secret of the Dispatcher project, for a staged (protected) deployment.
# VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET=
